########################################################################
# contreforts-declaration meta-shapes
#
# Part 2 of contreforts/contreforts-core#14. SHACL shapes that a connector
# declaration's *own* graph must satisfy, when that graph is loaded as DATA
# and validated against these shapes.
#
# Every rule here was checked against the two real declarations before
# being written down (contreforts-connector-forgejo's declaration.ttl,
# 103 triples; the O365 spike, 186 triples) -- not assumed from the issue's
# wording. One candidate from the issue text did NOT survive that check and
# was narrowed; see the comment on rule D14-META-4 below for what and why.
#
# What is deliberately NOT constrained here, and why:
#   - No sh:closed anywhere. A meta-shape that forbids predicates it
#     doesn't already know about would reject the third connector's
#     legitimate vocabulary for no reason -- exactly the failure mode the
#     issue warns against.
#   - No shape asserting sh:xone must have >= 2 alternatives, or that
#     alternative node shapes are well-formed beyond having paths (rule
#     META-3 already covers that generically). The cross-exclusion
#     structure itself is D14's job (lint::xone), not a SHACL shape --
#     SHACL cannot express a constraint on its own alternatives' mutual
#     exclusion (see contreforts-workspace#27 and the D14 lint).
#   - No constraint on contreforts:uiShape's value (e.g. an sh:in of known
#     component names). The component registry is not this crate's
#     concern, and enumerating it here would make every new UI component
#     a meta-shapes change.
#   - Nothing here enforces the core:-namespace alignment-stub rule (D2).
#     That check is graph-wide ("is this subject, wherever it appears,
#     something other than a SKOS alignment stub") rather than a per-node
#     SHACL constraint, and shacl-rust 0.2.9's SPARQL-based targeting is
#     exactly the kind of advanced-feature surface #21 flagged as
#     maturity risk. It is implemented as a Rust structural lint
#     (lint::core_ns) instead, for the same reason D14 is: reliability and
#     a message a connector author can act on without reading this file.
#     See the report on #14 for the exact carve-out chosen.
#   - Nothing here enforces "at least one sh:NodeShape carries
#     sh:targetClass" (the issue's first candidate). That is an
#     existential over the whole graph -- SHACL constraints are universal
#     over a resolved target set, so an *absence* of any matching node
#     resolves to zero focus nodes and vacuously conforms, not a
#     violation. Implemented as a Rust structural lint (lint::structural)
#     instead, for the same reason.
########################################################################

@prefix rdfs:        <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix sh:          <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd:         <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix contreforts: <https://contreforts.ds-labs.org/ontologies/declaration#> .
@prefix meta:        <https://contreforts.ds-labs.org/ontologies/declaration-meta#> .

########################################################################
# META-1 -- the connector's own node shape (the one carrying
# sh:targetClass) carries exactly one contreforts:category.
#
# Confirmed on both real files: forgejo:ForgejoConnectorShape and
# o365:O365ConnectorShape are each the sole sh:targetClass-bearing node
# shape in their file, and each carries exactly one contreforts:category
# ("git-forge", "calendar"). Neither the sh:xone alternatives
# (o365:ClientCredentialsShape, o365:DelegatedShape) nor nested shapes
# (forgejo:GroupMappingShape) carry sh:targetClass, so this rule does not
# reach them -- it targets only the shape(s) meta-shape META-META-1
# (the Rust structural lint) already requires to exist.
########################################################################

meta:TargetClassShapeHasCategory a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment "A connector's targeted node shape must carry exactly one contreforts:category." ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf sh:targetClass ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path contreforts:category ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A node shape with sh:targetClass must carry exactly one contreforts:category (catalog grouping for the connector picker)." ;
    ] .

########################################################################
# META-2 -- contreforts:secret, wherever used, is a single boolean.
#
# Confirmed on both files: o365:clientSecret/token/refreshToken and
# forgejo:token all carry `contreforts:secret true` as a single boolean
# literal.
########################################################################

meta:SecretIsBoolean a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment "contreforts:secret, wherever it is used, must be a single xsd:boolean." ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf contreforts:secret ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path contreforts:secret ;
        sh:datatype xsd:boolean ;
        sh:maxCount 1 ;
        sh:message "contreforts:secret must be a single xsd:boolean value." ;
    ] .

########################################################################
# META-3 -- every value of sh:property has a sh:path.
#
# Confirmed graph-wide on both files: every blank node reached via
# sh:property -- top-level connector fields, sh:xone alternatives' own
# property shapes, and forgejo:GroupMappingShape's nested fields alike --
# declares sh:path. This one genuinely holds everywhere, unlike META-4
# below, because it is a SHACL well-formedness minimum (a property shape
# without a path cannot resolve any value nodes at all) rather than
# presentation metadata that only some property shapes carry.
########################################################################

meta:PropertyShapeHasPath a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment "Every node reached via sh:property must declare sh:path." ;
    sh:targetObjectsOf sh:property ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sh:path ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:message "A property shape (reached via sh:property) must declare sh:path -- the UI and the D14 lint both need it to know which predicate this field is." ;
    ] .

########################################################################
# META-4 -- a connector's TOP-LEVEL property shapes carry sh:name, unless
# they are a pure D14 cross-exclusion stub (sh:maxCount 0).
#
# NARROWED FROM THE ISSUE'S WORDING. The issue's candidate reads "every
# non-exclusion property shape carries sh:name". Checked literally against
# the hardened O365 declaration, that is FALSE: o365:ClientCredentialsShape
# and o365:DelegatedShape each restate their variant's positive fields
# (authMode, tenantId, clientId, clientSecret / authMode, token,
# refreshToken) purely for sh:xone's cardinality check, and NONE of those
# inner restatements carry sh:name -- the presentation metadata
# (sh:name/sh:description/sh:order/sh:group) lives only on the outer, flat
# property shapes on o365:O365ConnectorShape itself. A meta-shape enforcing
# the issue's literal wording would reject the hardened declaration that
# the issue itself says must pass. This is reported as a finding on #14,
# not papered over.
#
# The rule actually implemented: sh:name is required on a property shape
# only when it is a DIRECT sh:property child of a node shape that itself
# carries sh:targetClass (i.e. the top-level, renderable field list) --
# and even then, not when that property shape is a pure exclusion stub.
# sh:xone alternatives are reached via sh:xone, not sh:property, from the
# targeted shape, so this rule does not walk into them at all; it never
# sees their unnamed, restated property shapes in the first place.
########################################################################

meta:TopLevelPropertyHasNameUnlessExclusion a sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:comment "A connector's own top-level property shapes carry sh:name, unless they are a bare D14 sh:maxCount-0 exclusion stub." ;
    sh:targetSubjectsOf sh:targetClass ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path sh:property ;
        sh:node [
            sh:or (
                [
                    sh:property [
                        sh:path sh:maxCount ;
                        sh:hasValue 0 ;
                    ]
                ]
                [
                    sh:property [
                        sh:path sh:name ;
                        sh:minCount 1 ;
                    ]
                ]
            ) ;
        ] ;
        sh:message "A top-level property shape must carry sh:name (the UI has nothing to render without it), unless it is a bare sh:maxCount 0 exclusion stub." ;
    ] .
